\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces The experimental contrasts. Each arrow changes exactly one phenomenon, so no comparison is confounded. The first three are the decomposition controls (topology $\times $ loss); the rest is the monotone fidelity ladder.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:contrasts}{{S1}{2}{}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Objective versus economic cost on the decomposition lineage (EUR/yr). The Gurobi objective carries a systematic $\sim $39--41\,\% non-economic residual that is \emph  {not} a dispatch distortion: the objective charges CO$_2$ on a gross basis whereas the reported cost applies the standard cogeneration self-use allocation ($\approx $55--58\,k), plus a thermal-storage cycling penalty ($\approx $24--26\,k). Both are near-constant across levels and cancel in every bias/regret difference, so all reported figures use the economic cost.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:objdecomp}{{S2}{2}{}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces Station-resolved hydraulics on Memmingen (real component data). The actual hydraulic pumping need is two orders of magnitude below the installed pump capacity, and every node keeps a large differential-pressure margin. Station-level hydraulics therefore move dispatch cost by $<1$\,\% and decisions by $\approx 0$.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hydraulics}{{S3}{2}{}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Solver-gap stability of the decomposition (R2.3 rigour). At the loose tolerance the original solves used, the small topology and interaction terms sat within solver noise; only at a $0.01$\,\% gap do they resolve to stable values. Loss dominance is far above the gap throughout.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:gapstability}{{S4}{2}{}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Robustness of the decomposition. The loss-dominance conclusion survives every modelling choice examined; where a quantity is sensitive (last-mile loss magnitude, value of temperature flexibility) it does not affect the ordering. Values are the loss main effect (\% of the gap) or the copperplate regret sign, as applicable.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:robustness}{{S5}{3}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces Out-of-sample prediction of the cost gap (loss burden) from observable network properties. The estimator is fitted on the synthetic networks up to 15\,km trunk length (train $R^2=0.999$) and tested on the held-out longer networks, an \emph  {extrapolation} beyond the fitted range. It predicts the 30\,km networks to within a few points and degrades at the extreme 50\,km extrapolation (held-out MAPE 19\,\%), which we report as given.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prediction}{{S6}{3}{}{table.6}{}}
